---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../components/TableScroll.astro'
import { Badge } from 'web-design-system/components'

const toc = [
  { label: 'How sync works', href: '#sync' },
  { label: 'Todoist', href: '#todoist' },
  { label: 'GitHub', href: '#github' },
  { label: 'The provider layer', href: '#providers' },
  { label: 'Tool servers, hooks, and proxies', href: '#mcp' },
  { label: 'The agent protocol', href: '#acp' },
  { label: 'Where each piece lives', href: '#where' },
]

const agentRuns = `cargo run -p agent-cli                    # interactive TUI
cargo run -p agent-cli -- --acp           # ACP server over stdio (JSON-RPC 2.0 NDJSON)
cargo run -p agent-cli -- -p "fix the tests"   # single-shot prompt, runs with tools`

const mcpConfig = `{
  "mcp_servers": [
    { "name": "my-tools", "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "."] }
  ],
  "hooks": [
    { "event": "on_turn_end", "command": "echo turn done" }
  ],
  "proxy": { "enabled": false, "url": "http://localhost:8317/v1" }
}`

const modules = [
  { surface: 'Todoist', code: 'apps/todo-2/src/todoist_auth.rs, libs/storage/src/todoist.rs, apps/todo-2/src/ui_parts/todoist_sync.rs', spec: 'docs/spec/todoist-sync-spec.md' },
  { surface: 'GitHub', code: 'apps/todo-2/src/github_auth.rs, libs/storage/src/github.rs', spec: 'docs/spec/github-integration-spec.md' },
  { surface: 'Coding workflow git side', code: 'apps/todo-2/src/coding_git.rs, apps/todo-2/src/coding_mcp.rs', spec: 'docs/spec/coding-workflow-runs-spec.md' },
  { surface: 'Agent panel ← ACP client', code: 'apps/todo-2/src/ui_parts/agent_pane.rs, libs/acp-client', spec: 'docs/spec/acp-client-and-agent-panel-spec.md' },
  { surface: 'Provider layer', code: 'libs/ai_providers, apps/agent-cli/src/providers.rs', spec: 'apps/agent-cli/README.md' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Integrations"
  description="The technical side of the outside world: how sync is mapped, where each piece lives, and the protocols the app speaks. Setting up your own accounts is in the User guide."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Setting this up for yourself">
    If you want to connect an account or pick a model rather than read the implementation, start at
    <a href="/configuration">Configuration</a>. This page is the internals.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="sync">How sync works</h2>
  <p>
    Sync is bolt-on, not load-bearing. The local database is authoritative for the app; a sync engine maps
    it onto an external system, records the mapping in the storage layer, and reconciles changes in both
    directions. Nothing in the task loop requires a network call, so a sync that fails leaves the app fully
    usable.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each outside system gets its own storage module, its own authentication path in the app, and a spec in
    <code>docs/spec/</code> that states what is implemented today — those specs are the accurate record,
    including the parts that are still open.
  </p>

  <h2 id="todoist">Todoist</h2>
  <p>
    Todoist is the primary external task list. The current spec (draft v1) covers import plus an immediate
    push of title, description, and done deltas; pushing every field is still open. Migrations for the
    mapping tables run through the storage crate's migration binary (<code>mig</code> in the dev shell).
  </p>

  <h2 id="github">GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub support is in preview and has two halves: issues and projects sync, and a git-backed coding
    workflow. <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">preview</Badge> Expect the shape of the mapping to
    change, since the sync engine and the workflow engine are both still moving.
  </p>
  <p>
    The coding workflow is the interesting half: a run can use a real checkout, drive the git operations
    itself, and reach tool servers over MCP (<code>coding_git.rs</code> and <code>coding_mcp.rs</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">The provider layer</h2>
  <p>
    Providers are OpenAI-compatible endpoints. <code>libs/ai_providers</code> implements them on top of the
    cersei agent library, including an OAuth-backed provider for services that do not hand out static keys.
    Request tuning is per model rather than per provider, so one model's sampling parameters can never leak
    into another model on the same endpoint.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fallback lives above the provider: a <em>combo</em> is a named sequence of provider/model pairs, tried
    in order when a run fails before producing any output. Because the switch only happens before the first
    token, a retry cannot duplicate partial work. Entries that fail cool down for a few minutes, and that
    cooldown state is persisted in a small JSON file rather than a database, so a rate-limited provider is
    not hammered again immediately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">Tool servers, hooks, and proxies</h2>
  <p>
    The agent's config also wires up its tools and its lifecycle. MCP servers are launched as child
    processes and their tools are merged into the agent's tool set; hooks run shell commands on agent
    events; a proxy can route provider traffic through a gateway.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename=".abstract/config.json" code={mcpConfig} />

  <h2 id="acp">The agent protocol</h2>
  <p>
    <code>agent-cli</code> doubles as an Agent Client Protocol server, which is how the desktop app's agent
    pane drives it. The protocol runs over stdio as newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 and supports{' '}
    <code>initialize</code>, <code>session/new</code>, <code>session/load</code>,{' '}
    <code>session/prompt</code>, <code>session/set_config_option</code>, and <code>session/cancel</code>,
    with streaming updates delivered as notifications.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={agentRuns} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    <code>session/new</code> advertises the available providers and models so a client can switch models
    mid-session, and a fallback switch is reported to the client rather than happening silently. The client
    side is <code>libs/acp-client</code>: process launch, sessions, the transcript model, and the
    file-system and terminal capabilities a session needs.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where each piece lives</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Where each integration surface lives">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Surface</th>
          <th>Code</th>
          <th>Spec</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          modules.map((entry) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{entry.surface}</td>
              <td><code>{entry.code}</code></td>
              <td><code>{entry.spec}</code></td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    <a href="/contributor/architecture">Architecture</a> covers the crates themselves, and the
    <a href="/contributor/architecture#specs">spec table</a> lists every spec with its status.
  </p>
</Docs>
